# Flying in Russian: лечу or летаю?
subtitle: "Лечу́ or лета́ю? Tom's parents fly in from London, and you learn one flight versus flying often, прилетя́т and улетя́т."
tags: ["russian","verbs of motion","лететь летать","flying in russian","russian grammar"]
teaches: ["grammar/verbs-of-motion","vocabulary/travel"]
thumb: "band \"One flight, or flying often?\""
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | Tom, a warm, easy-going English speaker who knows Russian well; friendly with Masha, clear and unhurried; says Russian words the way a native Russian speaker would","b":"gemini:Leda | a friendly young Russian woman from Moscow, natural and lively, speaks English with a light Russian accent and says Russian words natively; never exaggerated"}
next: {"title":"Someone and anything in Russian: кто-то, что-нибудь","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/russian-from-zero/117-russian-someone.cast"}

